1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,158
Last time, we gave every patient a
probability, added them up for Cedar,

2
00:00:05,198 --> 00:00:09,374
and got 8 expected readmissions.
Cedar had exactly 8.

3
00:00:09,517 --> 00:00:12,101
A perfect match. But a hollow one.

4
00:00:12,141 --> 00:00:17,590
Those probabilities were built from Cedar's
own results. We compared Cedar with itself.

5
00:00:17,733 --> 00:00:21,917
To judge a clinic, the expected number
has to come from somewhere else.

6
00:00:21,957 --> 00:00:25,870
So the real question is:
expected, according to whom?

7
00:00:27,367 --> 00:00:29,588
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

8
00:00:29,628 --> 00:00:32,346
Today: observed, versus expected.

9
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:40,010
Here's a first idea. Pour all five
clinics together: 103 patients.

10
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:43,715
Sort them by risk, and forget
which clinic they came from.

11
00:00:43,755 --> 00:00:49,219
13 of the 57 lower-risk
patients were readmitted: 23%.

12
00:00:49,259 --> 00:00:54,132
And 29 of the 46 higher-risk patients: 63%.

13
00:00:54,267 --> 00:00:59,417
Call that the pooled standard: what
happens to a typical patient of each kind.

14
00:01:00,817 --> 00:01:05,022
Now take Alder's 30 patients. On
the left, what happened to them.

15
00:01:05,062 --> 00:01:08,250
On the right, the slice the
standard gives each one.

16
00:01:08,383 --> 00:01:14,658
Add up the slices: 24 small ones
and 6 large ones come to about 9.3.

17
00:01:14,698 --> 00:01:16,406
That's Alder's expected count:

18
00:01:16,446 --> 00:01:20,232
what these same patients would
produce at a typical clinic.

19
00:01:20,367 --> 00:01:22,482
Alder actually had 13.

20
00:01:22,522 --> 00:01:30,232
That's 3.7 more than expected.
Or, as a ratio, 13 over 9.3: 1.4.

21
00:01:31,717 --> 00:01:34,286
Now do the same for all five clinics.

22
00:01:34,417 --> 00:01:42,447
Birch had 14, against 16.5 expected:
a ratio of 0.85. Fewer than expected.

23
00:01:42,583 --> 00:01:46,767
Remember the league table? Birch's
raw rate was higher than Alder's.

24
00:01:46,807 --> 00:01:51,914
Measured against what their own patients
were expected to do, Birch comes out ahead.

25
00:01:52,050 --> 00:01:57,727
And notice: the expected counts add
up to 42, exactly the number observed.

26
00:01:57,767 --> 00:02:02,149
Against a pooled standard, the
clinics as a whole always break even.

27
00:02:03,533 --> 00:02:07,360
It looks like we're done. But look
at where that standard came from.

28
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:12,310
Of the 57 lower-risk patients
in the pool, 24 are Alder's own.

29
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:16,063
And they supplied 8 of the 13 readmissions.

30
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:21,152
Take Alder out, and the lower-risk
rate drops from 23% to 15%.

31
00:02:21,192 --> 00:02:25,972
Alder has been measured against
a standard it pushed up itself.

32
00:02:26,012 --> 00:02:30,417
Against everyone else, its
ratio is not 1.4, but 1.8.

33
00:02:31,917 --> 00:02:35,568
There's a second, subtler
problem: the risk rule itself.

34
00:02:35,608 --> 00:02:40,096
In the pool, higher risk
multiplies the odds by 5.8.

35
00:02:40,233 --> 00:02:44,766
But compare patients inside the
same clinic, and the gap is wider:

36
00:02:44,806 --> 00:02:48,897
10 at Alder, 10 at Dune,
about 6 at Cedar and at Birch.

37
00:02:48,937 --> 00:02:51,676
Taken together, about seven and a half.

38
00:02:51,817 --> 00:02:56,364
Why the difference? Most higher-risk
patients are at Birch, which does well.

39
00:02:56,404 --> 00:02:59,884
Most lower-risk patients
are at Alder, which doesn't.

40
00:02:59,924 --> 00:03:03,922
The pool mixes up how sick patients
are with where they were treated.

41
00:03:05,417 --> 00:03:06,913
Let's gather it up.

42
00:03:06,953 --> 00:03:11,564
Expected is a sum of probabilities,
taken over a clinic's own patients.

43
00:03:11,604 --> 00:03:17,601
Observed minus expected is the evidence.
Observed over expected is the ratio.

44
00:03:17,641 --> 00:03:21,084
And both are only as fair
as the standard behind them.

45
00:03:21,233 --> 00:03:26,488
A fair standard needs two things: a risk rule
learned from patients who share a clinic,

46
00:03:26,528 --> 00:03:29,434
and a yardstick no single clinic can bend.

47
00:03:29,583 --> 00:03:35,074
Next time, we get both, with one small
change: every clinic gets a number of its own.

48
00:03:35,217 --> 00:03:39,322
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
